WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.790
Two weeks of rain readings!

2
00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:05.080
Every morning, my pupils read the gauge.

3
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.790
Now the garden club asks,

4
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.670
“Should we water today?”

5
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.830
And I’m staring at a page of numbers.

6
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.600
Today: how to record, chart and

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.260
use data to make a decision.

8
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.530
Data is facts we collect to

9
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.290
answer a question.

10
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.660
Our question: should we water the garden?

11
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.330
Readings alone won’t answer it.

12
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.320
We need four steps: record, chart,

13
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.580
spot the pattern, decide.

14
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.280
Step one: record.

15
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.520
A table with a row for each day,

16
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.240
and headings with units — date,

17
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.780
time, rain in millimetres.

18
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.030
Write zero for a dry day, and

19
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.190
“missed” if nobody read the gauge.

20
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.280
A blank could mean either.

21
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.030
Step two: chart.

22
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.790
A bar chart is a picture of data where

23
00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.390
each bar’s height shows a number — here,

24
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.310
the millimetres of rain each day.

25
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.940
Label the side in equal steps:

26
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.580
zero, five, ten, fifteen.

27
00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.570
You already do this with

28
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.430
the class register.

29
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.700
You tick names every morning.

30
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.650
Looking along a pupil’s row, you

31
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.190
notice: absent every Friday.

32
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.450
Then you decide what to do.

33
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.580
Record, spot, decide.

34
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:30.430
Quick check!

35
00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.370
On sports day, nobody read our rain gauge.

36
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.640
What should my pupils write

37
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.800
in Wednesday’s box?

38
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:39.680
“Missed.”

39
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.080
Zero would say no rain fell —

40
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.500
and nobody knows that.

41
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.700
Thursday’s reading then covers two days,

42
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.820
so note that beside the number.

43
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.380
Step three: spot the pattern.

44
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.040
Here: two wet days, then

45
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.480
five dry days in a row.

46
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.110
Step four: decide, using a rule the

47
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.150
class agreed — say, water if less

48
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.310
than five millimetres fell in three

49
00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.130
days and the soil feels dry.

50
00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.050
Today, we water.

51
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.310
Connected devices — the Internet of Things

52
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.450
— do the same steps automatically.

53
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.260
A soil sensor can take a reading every few

54
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.360
minutes and send it over a network.

55
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.080
A computer stores the readings, draws the

56
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.940
chart, and a rule can switch on a pump.

57
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:30.660
Your turn.

58
00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.850
Our new garden sensor

59
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.330
says the soil is wet.

60
00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.630
But the tomato leaves are drooping, and

61
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.310
it hasn’t rained for five days.

62
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:40.870
What should I do?

63
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.730
Check before you trust it: feel the soil

64
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.450
by the plants and look at the probe — it

65
00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.770
may sit in a damp spot, or be faulty.

66
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.010
Machines help, but people still check.

67
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.740
Remember: record, chart, spot

68
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.040
the pattern, decide.

69
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.650
Try it in class tomorrow: put a week

70
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:02.990
of rain readings on the board.

71
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.640
Pupils draw the bar chart together,

72
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.620
find the pattern, and agree a rule

73
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.860
for watering the garden.
